1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,542
The Map of Observing the Mysteries of the Heaven and Earth (Liangyi xuanlan tu 兩儀玄覽圖),

2
00:00:05,542 --> 00:00:11,479
which we call here the Xuantu, is a worldmap in eight panels created in 1603

3
00:00:11,479 --> 00:00:16,917
by the Ming dynasty military official Li Yingshi李應試.

4
00:00:16,917 --> 00:00:21,776
There is strong reason to believe that the Manchus obtained this particular map before

5
00:00:21,776 --> 00:00:27,093
they conquered China, a surprising development that makes us reconsider the Manchus'

6
00:00:27,093 --> 00:00:30,292
relationship to Western cartography.

7
00:00:30,292 --> 00:00:36,501
Matteo Ricci and the scholar Li Zhizao 李之藻 created in 1602 a map named Complete

8
00:00:36,501 --> 00:00:40,807
Map of the Myriad Kingdoms of the World (Kunyu Wanguo Quantu 坤輿萬國全圖).

9
00:00:40,807 --> 00:00:46,008
It included geographical descriptions of the historical five continents, complete with

10
00:00:46,008 --> 00:00:52,382
place-names, a précis of Ptolemaic cosmology, and paper instruments for measuring polar

11
00:00:52,382 --> 00:00:54,875
altitude and latitude.

12
00:00:54,875 --> 00:01:01,853
The Xuantu is a large object, measuring 2 meters vertically and 4.42 meters

13
00:01:01,853 --> 00:01:07,605
horizontally. Maps of this size meant for a high-end audience of officials were rarely

14
00:01:07,605 --> 00:01:14,071
prints. Instead, just as for paintings, Chinese artisans normally used silk, which was

15
00:01:14,071 --> 00:01:17,375
favored for its superior durability.

16
00:01:17,375 --> 00:01:23,372
For Sino-Western world maps, the desire to quickly disseminate many copies trumped that

17
00:01:23,372 --> 00:01:29,167
of creating solid, durable objects. They were as large in size as silk maps made

18
00:01:29,167 --> 00:01:34,194
for important people, yet easily reproducible and affordable.

19
00:01:34,194 --> 00:01:37,968
This was at the root of their success in the late Ming.

20
00:01:37,968 --> 00:01:41,950
It is also the reason why so few copies survived:

21
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:44,875
large prints were fragile.

22
00:01:44,875 --> 00:01:49,438
Indeed, Xuantu is important not so much for what it has in it but for

23
00:01:49,438 --> 00:01:55,917
what it has on it: the repairs, modifications, and annotations added to the original print.

24
00:01:55,917 --> 00:01:59,277
The Shenyang copy was originally uncolored.

25
00:01:59,277 --> 00:02:04,815
Yet during its life in the Shenyang palace, an artisan was tasked with embellishing the

26
00:02:04,815 --> 00:02:10,353
mountain ranges across all the continents in the style of Chinese painting.

27
00:02:10,353 --> 00:02:16,100
The color scheme of these decorations, blue, green, and gold, fits the one used

28
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:18,750
throughout the Shenyang palace.

29
00:02:18,750 --> 00:02:24,318
Besides the repairs and added decorative elements, the Shenyang copy of the Map of

30
00:02:24,318 --> 00:02:28,535
Observing Mysteries displays a number of Manchu annotations.

31
00:02:28,535 --> 00:02:33,586
The theory of the map expounded in the preface by Matteo Ricci presented relationships

32
00:02:33,586 --> 00:02:39,054
between Earth's spherical shape, its position in the solar system, and the cycles of

33
00:02:39,054 --> 00:02:44,105
night and day. All of these features, represented by lines on the map, had Manchu

34
00:02:44,105 --> 00:02:46,583
translations attached.

35
00:02:46,583 --> 00:02:52,317
Taken together, they suggest that the Manchu translator had a grasp of and an interest

36
00:02:52,317 --> 00:02:59,375
in the “theory” of the map, having read and understood the prefaces and other accompanying texts.

37
00:02:59,375 --> 00:03:03,992
When it was used in the Shenyang palace, the Xuantu was damaged.

38
00:03:03,992 --> 00:03:08,195
Winters in Shenyang are bitterly cold, and rooms must be heated.

39
00:03:08,195 --> 00:03:12,688
For this reason, there was a temperature difference between the upper and lower parts of

40
00:03:12,688 --> 00:03:17,947
the room. This created condensation, and because the map was most likely displayed

41
00:03:17,947 --> 00:03:23,292
vertically, the dampness damaged the portions closer to the ground.

42
00:03:23,292 --> 00:03:28,810
An artisan had applied paper paste and, after it dried, redrew the meridians to give

43
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:34,452
the impression of continuity. Further damage to the map reveals the discrepancy between

44
00:03:34,452 --> 00:03:36,333
the two layers.

45
00:03:36,333 --> 00:03:41,045
The amount of information included in the reconstruction on the top stratum differed

46
00:03:41,045 --> 00:03:42,984
from that of the layer below.

47
00:03:42,984 --> 00:03:46,652
This was due perhaps to the artisan's limited knowledge.

48
00:03:46,652 --> 00:03:50,948
But it might also be a reflection of the interests of the map owners.

49
00:03:50,948 --> 00:03:56,150
The Southern Hemisphere on the left side of the map (roll one), for instance,

50
00:03:56,150 --> 00:04:00,144
disappeared completely, being covered up in the restoration;

51
00:04:00,144 --> 00:04:05,994
only a circle and some of the radiating meridians were copied on top for consistency.

52
00:04:05,994 --> 00:04:11,231
On the other hand, the northern hemisphere was preserved, and annotations were added to

53
00:04:11,231 --> 00:04:16,000
it because it encompassed places the Manchus were interested in.

54
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:21,965
Most translations of place-names are concentrated in East Asia, radiating toward Europe.

55
00:04:21,965 --> 00:04:27,259
The Manchus, it seems, were primarily preoccupied with locating themselves and their

56
00:04:27,259 --> 00:04:34,125
immediate surroundings; in terms of their outward interest, they focused on looking westward.

57
00:04:34,125 --> 00:04:38,261
The translation of Mount Changbai (Changbai Shan長白山) is more revealing of the Manchus'

58
00:04:38,261 --> 00:04:42,615
own emerging sense of identity in the sixteenth century.

59
00:04:42,615 --> 00:04:48,245
The mountain, appearing here in translation as Sanggiyan Alin, was part of an imperial

60
00:04:48,245 --> 00:04:53,190
cult throughout the Qing, revered as the birthplace of the mythical founder of the

61
00:04:53,190 --> 00:04:59,007
dynasty, Bukūri Yongšon. The addition of an inscription alongside the mountain is an

62
00:04:59,007 --> 00:05:05,125
indication that the place was meaningful for the Manchus as early as the Later Jin.

63
00:05:05,125 --> 00:05:10,729
Moving beyond this sphere, immediately neighboring countries such as Japan, Korea, and

64
00:05:10,729 --> 00:05:15,153
the Great Ming are labeled using names of Manchu origin:

65
00:05:15,153 --> 00:05:24,208
Ose, Sohlo, and Nikan. These names do not describe places or countries but, rather, ethnicities.

66
00:05:24,208 --> 00:05:30,159
The rest of the translations of place-names on the map are transliterations in Manchu of

67
00:05:30,159 --> 00:05:35,515
the pronunciation of Chinese characters. The selection of these places cannot be easily

68
00:05:35,515 --> 00:05:40,042
explained by what we readily know about the Manchus.

69
00:05:40,042 --> 00:05:45,242
The translations of places like the Fortunate Islands, the Cape of Good Hope,

70
00:05:45,242 --> 00:05:50,641
Madagascar, Java, and the Philippines resulted from an awareness of the centers of the

71
00:05:50,641 --> 00:05:57,340
European trade network around Eurasia. These were, after all, places from which ships

72
00:05:57,340 --> 00:06:01,042
dropping anchor at Macao would have been coming.

73
00:06:01,042 --> 00:06:05,916
According to the Manchu Veritable Records, the Manchu written language was a direct

74
00:06:05,916 --> 00:06:12,132
invention of bilingual scholars working for Nurhaci and was established in 1599.

75
00:06:12,132 --> 00:06:19,250
The script could not clearly distinguish a and e, o and u, or k, g, and h.

76
00:06:19,250 --> 00:06:24,442
A reform of the language that would address these issues was implemented during the time

77
00:06:24,442 --> 00:06:30,340
of the second Khan, Hong Taiji. The most important and visible aspect of this reform

78
00:06:30,340 --> 00:06:35,625
was the addition of dots and circles to solve the problem of distinction.

79
00:06:35,625 --> 00:06:40,116
Analysis of the form of the script on the map thus indicates that the Map

80
00:06:40,116 --> 00:06:47,256
of Observing Mysteries was translated between roughly 1625 and 1636, when the reformed

81
00:06:47,256 --> 00:06:50,472
version of the Old Manchu script was used.

82
00:06:50,472 --> 00:06:54,906
In any case, the map's annotations indicate an interest in, as well as an

83
00:06:54,906 --> 00:07:01,547
epistemological engagement with, a Sino-Western artifact in a crucial moment, as the

84
00:07:01,547 --> 00:07:05,750
Manchus were beginning to plan their conquest of China.

85
00:07:05,750 --> 00:07:10,599
This section of the map of the Great Ming depicts the western portion of Korea

86
00:07:10,599 --> 00:07:15,823
and displays the Manchu place-name transliterations on affixed labels.

87
00:07:15,823 --> 00:07:22,375
In some places, over time, the labels have detached, revealing the original Chinese text underneath.

88
00:07:22,375 --> 00:07:28,250
Repairs, modifications, as well as annotations therefore, all belonged to a broad

89
00:07:28,250 --> 00:07:33,928
strategy of translation of cartographic artifacts first practiced in the preconquest

90
00:07:33,928 --> 00:07:39,920
period on the map by Li Yingshi and Ricci, and ultimately, after the conquest, on

91
00:07:39,920 --> 00:07:47,000
Ming maps. This demonstrates the continuity in translation across the Ming-Qing divide.

92
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:52,206
The presence of a map co-authored by Matteo Ricci in the Shenyang palace is ultimately

93
00:07:52,206 --> 00:08:00,000
a testament to the extent of knowledge networks in the early modern period.
